Csaba: So, the other one, which is called, 8077.

347
00:51:01.880 --> 00:51:07.480
Csaba: Which is the, so what's the nada?

348
00:51:08.270 --> 00:51:23.730
Csaba: In demand pool for transactions, we basically now have two levels of messaging. One is at the level of the whole transaction. The other is just at the level of transaction hashes, which has no information at all about what that transaction is.

349
00:51:23.930 --> 00:51:29.079
Csaba: We have a little bit of metadata about the type and the size, but that's it.

350
00:51:29.440 --> 00:51:33.319
Csaba: That still does nothing about what that transaction is.

351
00:51:33.550 --> 00:51:38.059
Csaba: And that, creates, problems.

352
00:51:38.970 --> 00:51:44.249
Csaba: if and when we want to scale the mempool. Now, if we want to go up with, with…

353
00:51:44.370 --> 00:51:45.460
Csaba: Lukasz…

354
00:51:48.010 --> 00:51:54.479
Csaba: We might also want to scale the public mempool. I've seen different opinions on this, but I would like to have a public mempool, which is…

355
00:51:54.570 --> 00:52:00.179
Csaba: Scaling nicely with these numbers. And then, we have the problem that

356
00:52:00.200 --> 00:52:19.050
Csaba: nodes only see hashes, and have no idea what's behind those hashes, so either they randomly pull hashes, or they randomly pull hashes. They don't have any other choice. So what is proposed here is to add, besides the hash, add some metadata to the announcements.

357
00:52:19.870 --> 00:52:25.839
Csaba: So that nodes can do more intelligence, decisions in the mempore logic.

358
00:52:26.450 --> 00:52:33.499
Csaba: I think it's needed for scaling, let's see what people think.

359
00:52:40.600 --> 00:52:53.829
Csaba: I just mentioned metadata, there are different variants here. Metadata can be, nonsense, just nonsense cannot be. It's… it can be access and loans. There were also discussions on including fees or not.

360
00:52:54.030 --> 00:53:09.459
Csaba: It's also to be said that there are small transactions, where, if you add this metadata, you are basically having all the content, so at that point, you would just push the transaction. That is also that transaction size. So this is… what makes sense is very much transaction size dependent.

361
00:53:14.750 --> 00:53:21.839
Csaba: I see lots of texts in the chat, but if, yeah, if there are questions, actually, happy to answer. Otherwise…

362
00:53:22.080 --> 00:53:24.900
Csaba: We can move on, and I will lead to the chat.

363
00:53:38.350 --> 00:53:42.389
nixo: Nobody wants to… Ask any questions, we'll move on.

364
00:53:43.690 --> 00:53:52.950
nixo: To… Danno's, proposed EIP8355 precompiles for ML DSA verification.

365
00:53:54.980 --> 00:53:58.099
Danno Ferrin: And, where's chat? Let me post this.

366
00:53:58.350 --> 00:54:00.060
Danno Ferrin: Nope, wrong window.

367
00:54:08.070 --> 00:54:10.240
Danno Ferrin: Right window, that's better.

368
00:54:12.610 --> 00:54:17.360
Danno Ferrin: And… here's… Presentation link.

369
00:54:17.900 --> 00:54:25.900
Danno Ferrin: So I'm proposing, MLDSA precompiles, I'm proposing just the simple FIPS 204 variant of it, which is the one that basically you're gonna find

370
00:54:26.290 --> 00:54:28.749
Danno Ferrin: Very easily places to implement it.

371
00:54:30.330 --> 00:54:46.299
Danno Ferrin: So my first part of the argument is that MLDSA is the most widely used, by far, post-quantum cryptography algorithm today, and it probably will be for years. It's been finalized in August. There was a competitor that would be really great for blockchain called Falcon. Unfortunately, that is stuck in

372
00:54:46.300 --> 00:54:58.399
Danno Ferrin: standards never, never land. Who knows if it'll get out. And the other alternative, SLHDSA, is only recommended for firmware signing, and my understanding is that the XMSS variants are much more favored within Ethereum there.

373
00:54:59.930 --> 00:55:13.959
Danno Ferrin: The cloud management systems on Google and, Amazon already have full support for MLDSA. Azure and Oracle are lagging, but there is external solutions such as Thales that you can use.

374
00:55:14.070 --> 00:55:23.000
Danno Ferrin: But another big thing is hardware security modules. If you don't want to use cloud, if you want to plug something into your computer, those are the big names for HSMs that you can plug into your computer and get.

375
00:55:23.160 --> 00:55:33.440
Danno Ferrin: But I think a more telling thing of how widely used it is, TLS 1.3 uses it, X509 uses it, WebAuthN, the current draft, uses it, and they use the FIPS 402 formulations.

376
00:55:33.870 --> 00:55:43.219
Danno Ferrin: So from this, this is probably the best news for people implementing this, is clients should not be writing this cryptography. Every single client language out there, and ones that would be relevant.

377
00:55:43.320 --> 00:56:01.070
Danno Ferrin: have MLDSA built in, or by the time Hagita ships, we'll have it built in. Goes is still in the release candidate.net and Java ship it in the baseline. Rust has some really high-quality libraries, although they might not be core. The reference implementations in C. Node.js has put it in a 24-70.

378
00:56:01.090 --> 00:56:08.780
Danno Ferrin: And it's starting to show up in browser natives. You can, of course, compile your Rust program into, into…

379
00:56:09.200 --> 00:56:15.939
Danno Ferrin: web… into the various JavaScript web formats. It's kind of big, so you'll want to use the native format there.

380
00:56:16.080 --> 00:56:23.129
Danno Ferrin: And if we're using the standard that the rest of the world uses, we don't have to worry about corner cases like what happened with Kachak.

381
00:56:23.310 --> 00:56:31.359
Danno Ferrin: So we don't have to bring our own cryptography with it, we just use what exists in place, so we don't have to bring megabytes of our, of our,

382
00:56:31.620 --> 00:56:33.090
Danno Ferrin: of our WASM code.

383
00:56:33.360 --> 00:56:45.980
Danno Ferrin: Interop test vectors already exist for these because it's the standard, and they include the type of implementation errors that, caught us in the early days when we were implementing our own, ZCCP256K1.

384
00:56:46.150 --> 00:56:59.879
Danno Ferrin: With ones where they had, you know, tests that were multiple things were wrong, but thought they were testing only one. So these test vectors that they have available test these common implementation errors. They're properly isolated, so that if you don't have all the corner cases, you're going to fail something.

385
00:57:00.710 --> 00:57:10.470
Danno Ferrin: So, the only new tests we'd really need to write are tests that we know very well how to write, precompile bindings and gas costing. Those are things that we are very well experienced with.

386
00:57:10.970 --> 00:57:26.870
Danno Ferrin: And finally, there are some divergences. There was a prior MLDSA precompile proposal, but there are some key things that I diverged within this. The biggest thing being is a theme, I am sticking with FIPS 204. If a decision is a FIPS 204 thing, we go with it.

387
00:57:26.870 --> 00:57:42.150
Danno Ferrin: We use the compressed key, not the 20K expanded key. We provide all security levels, not just MLDSA44. It's important to note that this is a hedge in case there's more crypto analytic research that brings the security bits down. They keep hacking at it.

388
00:57:42.300 --> 00:57:56.069
Danno Ferrin: Yeah, nothing's brought it down below level 2 yet, but, you know, if we're going to put stuff in here for potentially 10, 15 years, we need to be aware of that. We're also using the full-width message. Unlike, MLDSA, unlike the SCP,

389
00:57:56.180 --> 00:57:59.220
Danno Ferrin: Unlike the ECICS ones,

390
00:57:59.280 --> 00:58:12.979
Danno Ferrin: it hashes over the whole message, not just the hash of it. The hashing is built into it. So the API in this particular DIP, accepts messages of arbitrary size, but in practice, we're still going to assign TX hashes, so we don't have to use the full-size messages.

391
00:58:13.020 --> 00:58:25.120
Danno Ferrin: And finally, only the FIPS hash functions are used, so it's going to be all internal to these libraries. We're not trying to make an exploded version of this that we can run inside the EVM. This is meant to be a precompile.

392
00:58:25.390 --> 00:58:30.010
Danno Ferrin: So those are, you know, in the interest of time, those are the high-speed three-level arguments, so I think

393
00:58:30.040 --> 00:58:48.240
Danno Ferrin: now's the right time for an MLDS precompile. And, of course, this can be used for 8141, it can be used for, online DAOs, as a substitute for EC Recover. I'm not proposing that we put it directly in the transaction formats, I think that's a bit too far, so…

394
00:58:54.330 --> 00:59:02.690
Danno Ferrin: So, Ansgar said, really low context comment, but the fact there was a power EIP indicates there's a non-trivial design space. How would we make sure that the version we ship

395
00:59:02.710 --> 00:59:21.060
Danno Ferrin: now is the optimal one. So that design space was mostly focusing on the idea of implementing MLDSA in raw EVM bytes. And I think it was a valuable experiment to go through, because it cost, you know, millions, you know, 5 to 9 million, if we used it as specified. So the design space there.

396
00:59:21.120 --> 00:59:34.269
Danno Ferrin: Shows that some of the compatibility just, you know, we would need to have a SHA-3, not a cache, and that would, you know, solve a lot of the problems there. But the EVM is just not set up for bit twiddling.

397
00:59:37.350 --> 00:59:46.879
Danno Ferrin: Kevandri asks, it's not clear to me that we need 3 new precompiles for different signature variants of different security levels. Not everyone is comfortable with security level 2.

398
00:59:47.110 --> 00:59:54.779
Danno Ferrin: some people are going to want the higher level security levels. I think the only reason we only have one security level in VC Recover is because our,

399
00:59:55.120 --> 01:00:05.380
Danno Ferrin: The, the size of the registers in the stack was 256 bits, and the way you get more security in elliptic curve is you go up to 384 bits, and we thrashed for years trying to figure out how to do that.

400
01:00:05.380 --> 01:00:21.859
Danno Ferrin: So by bringing it as a precompile, I think we get it day one, and it's clear how we can use it. Some people are going to want to use, the 65 or the 87 variant instead of the 44. For a while there, Google didn't even ship MLDSA44 in their trials.

401
01:00:23.800 --> 01:00:25.460
Danno Ferrin: And Justin has his hand up.

402
01:00:25.890 --> 01:00:44.159
Justin Florentine (Besu): I noticed that there was a section in there on not including the hash inside the algorithm, and with, like, a lot of this PQ stuff, it's all about the space, as far as, like, how do we wedge this into Ethereum with all the space that increases. Are there any other aspects of this that allow us to control the size of these signatures?

403
01:00:44.290 --> 01:00:46.539
Justin Florentine (Besu): Are the output of the, precompiles?

404
01:00:48.660 --> 01:01:06.690
Danno Ferrin: So the output of the precompile is going to be pass-fail. It's the message size, and the signature size, and the key size. So the biggest thing that this precompile does that the old 8051 does, is we use the FIPS 204 encoding, which is on the order of, like, you know, 2, 3, 4 kilobytes.

405
01:01:06.690 --> 01:01:13.119
Danno Ferrin: Rather than 20 kilobytes. And the reason that expansion was done in 8054 is to remove a bunch of bit twiddling.

406
01:01:13.160 --> 01:01:22.339
Danno Ferrin: In verifying the signature. The EVM is not terribly efficient for large-scale ships into odd-sized, bit sizes.

407
01:01:22.530 --> 01:01:27.470
Danno Ferrin: So that was, you know, that was an optimization done to facilitate EVM.

408
01:01:27.620 --> 01:01:31.560
Danno Ferrin: But as far as… as far as the sizes,

409
01:01:32.370 --> 01:01:51.160
Danno Ferrin: I have a… one thing we could do is if there's ways to store the public key on-chain, you could do validations by just providing a signature. I have, unrelated to this, I have a change to the set delegate opcode that would allow you to set data on that, and you could set your public key there. Or you could just deploy a contract there.

410
01:01:51.340 --> 01:01:57.359
Danno Ferrin: Storing it in SLOADStore data is going to be kind of expensive, because you've got to break it down into 32-byte sections.

411
01:01:57.560 --> 01:01:59.379
Danno Ferrin: Which is problematic.

412
01:02:00.290 --> 01:02:03.409
Justin Florentine (Besu): Thanks. I'm interested in that public key storage, I'll talk to you offline.

