01:11:41.790 --> 01:11:42.830
nixo: op codes.

488
01:11:43.680 --> 01:12:03.519
@ritzdorf / Hubert Ritzdorf (EIP-8219): Yeah, hi everyone. So, basically, the most basic, DFI operation, I would argue, is, like, an addition, and if you insidity you do A plus B. As most of you are probably aware, on EVM level, you're doing something way more complicated, because, you're doing these, safe mass checks.

489
01:12:03.580 --> 01:12:15.040
@ritzdorf / Hubert Ritzdorf (EIP-8219): So, what this EIP is basically proposing is that, so much great optimization work has been done, but, we can also try to optimize what is commonly being used by smart contracts.

490
01:12:15.170 --> 01:12:25.130
@ritzdorf / Hubert Ritzdorf (EIP-8219): As in, introduce, safe opcodes. So, opcodes that do safe addition, safe subtraction, safe multiplication, safe division.

491
01:12:25.170 --> 01:12:43.210
@ritzdorf / Hubert Ritzdorf (EIP-8219): So, as an example, there's the definition of safe addition here, where you compute the result, and if the result indicates that you did an overflow, you revert with 0, and otherwise, you just continue, so you basically

492
01:12:43.300 --> 01:12:44.950
@ritzdorf / Hubert Ritzdorf (EIP-8219): Have an addition.

493
01:12:45.120 --> 01:12:56.450
@ritzdorf / Hubert Ritzdorf (EIP-8219): If we, check what this… if we kind of, look what this does when we compare to, kind of, Solidity and Viper, with optimization enabled.

494
01:12:56.450 --> 01:13:08.790
@ritzdorf / Hubert Ritzdorf (EIP-8219): first of all, what we're saving is we're saving a lot of execution gas, because, like, on Solidity currently, one of these editions costs, 79 gas, wipe a bit less.

495
01:13:09.190 --> 01:13:16.289
@ritzdorf / Hubert Ritzdorf (EIP-8219): So we have, like, a, very significant, execution, gas reduction,

496
01:13:16.710 --> 01:13:24.990
@ritzdorf / Hubert Ritzdorf (EIP-8219): For every addition, plus we are reducing bytecode size, plus we are reducing deployment costs, because we are reducing bytecode size.

497
01:13:25.260 --> 01:13:47.960
@ritzdorf / Hubert Ritzdorf (EIP-8219): So when I first, when, yeah, so why are we not doing certain things? Just maybe really quickly. So, the goal is really to have a single opcode for this checked arithmetic. So, there were previous, proposals, for example, to introduce an overflow bit or something like that, but then you still need to branch on that. It doesn't really, kind of solve the problem from our perspective.

