658
01:36:30.850 --> 01:36:34.759
lightclient: I think it is just, like, a bit difficult to reach…

659
01:36:35.140 --> 01:36:41.109
lightclient: You know, total consensus on this topic, because it's obviously a topic that has existed for a very long time.

660
01:36:41.270 --> 01:36:49.869
lightclient: And people have different opinions on it. And I think, you know, we're arguing from different perspectives. Like, BASE has already shipped 8130.

661
01:36:50.120 --> 01:36:58.170
lightclient: And the mainnet is not shipped anything yet. And I think that, like, base always has the…

662
01:36:58.350 --> 01:37:09.049
lightclient: you know, the incentive to argue, to deliver the thing that they've kind of already delivered and have invested the resources in, whereas, like, L1 has different incentivization.

663
01:37:09.430 --> 01:37:10.340
lightclient: So…

664
01:37:10.930 --> 01:37:21.780
lightclient: at the end of the day, like, we need to do what is best on L1, and I want it to be something that is supported across L2s, and I want to do what we need in frame so that people are happy.

665
01:37:21.930 --> 01:37:30.489
lightclient: But at the end of the day, like, it's gonna… you know, everyone's gonna be a bit unhappy. You know, that's just the way that controversial EIPs end up.

666
01:37:30.780 --> 01:37:32.570
lightclient: And so we need to be ready for that.

667
01:37:33.500 --> 01:37:40.849
lightclient: And, you know, we spend a lot of time to get client teams to support frames, and there has been a lot of concessions and a lot of iterations.

668
01:37:41.340 --> 01:37:45.169
lightclient: So I don't want to, like, lose the progress that we've made here.

669
01:37:45.350 --> 01:37:47.840
lightclient: And not change anything for Ethereum.

670
01:37:49.180 --> 01:37:55.839
Chris - Base: Yeah, I'll just add, like, I think, everyone does want,

671
01:37:56.190 --> 01:38:08.349
Chris - Base: native A on L1, and I, like, the long-term Ethereum goals, I'm very aligned with, and I, you know, I do want that. Like, no A, I would rather have 8141 on L1, absolutely.

672
01:38:08.600 --> 01:38:26.930
Chris - Base: But I just, like, genuinely believe 8130 is a… can satisfy the same long-term goals in a way that, ultimately is, like, more performant and, doesn't make compromises, that are hard to come back from.

673
01:38:27.320 --> 01:38:37.949
Chris - Base: I believe, like, having the validation explicit, structurally in the transaction type does allow for just structural optimizations throughout clients,

674
01:38:38.380 --> 01:38:44.700
Chris - Base: And we'll continue to, like, even as we roll out maybe new validation mechanisms,

675
01:38:45.410 --> 01:38:48.429
Chris - Base: It's much easier to have that explicit in the transaction.

